{
  "title": "N-Queens",
  "description": "Place `n` queens on an `n \u00d7 n` chessboard so that no two queens attack each other: no two share a row, a column, or a diagonal.\n\nReturn **every** distinct solution. Represent a board as a list of `n` strings, one per row from top to bottom, where `'Q'` marks a queen and `'.'` marks an empty square.\n\n### Examples\n\n| `n` | Result |\n| --- | --- |\n| `4` | `[[\".Q..\", \"...Q\", \"Q...\", \"..Q.\"], [\"..Q.\", \"Q...\", \"...Q\", \".Q..\"]]` |\n| `1` | `[[\"Q\"]]` |\n| `3` | `[]` (no solution exists) |\n\n### Constraints\n\n- `1 <= n <= 8`\n- Return the solutions in **ascending lexicographic order**, comparing boards row by row as strings. The checker compares your output exactly.\n- Inputs are passed as function arguments and the answer is returned from the function. Do not read from standard input or print the result.\n",
  "reference": "void place(int row, int n, vector<int>& cols, vector<vector<string>>& out) {\n    if (row == n) {\n        vector<string> board(n, string(n, '.'));\n        for (int r = 0; r < n; ++r) {\n            board[r][cols[r]] = 'Q';\n        }\n        out.push_back(board);\n        return;\n    }\n    for (int c = 0; c < n; ++c) {\n        bool ok = true;\n        for (int r = 0; r < row && ok; ++r) {\n            if (cols[r] == c || abs(cols[r] - c) == row - r) {\n                ok = false;\n            }\n        }\n        if (ok) {\n            cols[row] = c;\n            place(row + 1, n, cols, out);\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nvector<vector<string>> solve_n_queens(int n) {\n    vector<vector<string>> out;\n    vector<int> cols(n, -1);\n    place(0, n, cols, out);\n    sort(out.begin(), out.end());\n    return out;\n}\n",
  "template": "vector<vector<string>> solve_n_queens(int n) {\n    // Return every valid board, in ascending lexicographic order.\n    return {};\n}\n",
